<https://w3id.org/EVI#supports> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The supports property is a transitive relation entailed by its non-transitive subproperty directlySupports. Typical nodes are DigitalObjects; provenance shortcuts (used, generatedBy, createdBy, associatedWith) also participate so warrant can propagate through Activities and Agents. If A supports B, the correctness of A is a warrant for belief in the correctness of B." .
<https://w3id.org/EVI> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "The Evidence Graph Ontology (EVI) is an OWL 2 vocabulary for recording how a biomedical result was produced and what evidence currently bears on its correctness. A published finding does not stand alone: it often relies on a dataset, a description of that dataset's schema, the software and computation that transformed the data, and the article that reports the result. EVI models those objects and the relations among them as an evidence graph from which a person or a program can follow how the source data, software, and computation support the finding. EVI is a biomedical domain extension of W3C PROV-O, built on PROV-O's core classes. Where PROV-O supplies general provenance for what was used, generated, and attributed, EVI adds the concepts needed in biomedical research and represents their relations as evidence for a published result, claim, or finding. When new evidence, assertions, or other information about the data, software, claim, or article become available, they can be added to the same graph as annotations, so both current and past evidence remain visible and transparent. The resulting graphs improve interpretability of biomedical research and provide the provenance layer required for pre-model AI-readiness."@en .
<https://w3id.org/EVI#Computation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity> .
